A||N||8||Blue Monster||leaky trans lines||||1||
Z||000000||Blue Monster||07-09-2000||02:47 AM||||I have a blue 87 GT. It has been wrecked before but the frame has been fixed. I need to replace the transmission cooling line, (at least thats what I think it is). Its old and leaks just under the driver side door near a line clamp. I have not been under the car to really get a look at it. Are the lines a special part? Or can I make them. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||07-09-2000||02:52 AM||||Im really not very knowledgeable about transmissions, but I believe the line that runs just under the door(on both sides) is the coolant line for the engine...runs from radiator to engine, back to radiator, so on and so on...not sure about a transmission line being there, but I could be wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000002||vinnywolfe||07-09-2000||10:22 AM||||i have an 87 gt and have noticed my tranny line leaking in the same area, right underneath/behind the driver's door.  i had the same question you did about replacing it, so i will be monitoring this post for any info.<P>vinny<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||07-09-2000||11:05 AM||||The tranny cooling lines go into the radiator on the driver's side. I think they run back to the rear on that same side too. Be carefully tracking them down, they look similar to brake lines and I think they run back along the driver's side too. I'd remove the whole line and replace it with a new steel line.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Boxdin||07-09-2000||03:48 PM||||I recently replaced both trans cool rubber lines after my 88 Formula was tapped in a parking lot hit. The hit knocked one ajar and I sprayed trans fluid on a hot rear brake disc. Looked and smelled far worse than it was.  Easy fix, new fuel line hose and clamps.  Forget the size, 3/8's or something like that.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||07-09-2000||05:59 PM||||DO NOT use rubber fuel line to repair tranny cooling lines. The tranny fluid is under a lot of pressure and will eventually cause the rubber hose to deteriorate. If you are going to just repair a small section, then use high pressure hose like for hydraulics or A/C systems. Some auto parts stores even stock tranny hose for use with a remote cooler. Again, DO NOT use fuel hose...I'm speaking from experience on this one, trust me.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Blue Monster||07-09-2000||08:30 PM||||I will be working on it this comming weekend. I will let all you know how it goes. Thanks for the tips.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||07-10-2000||10:42 PM||||What bat's said, DON'T use fuel line for tranny line. It's both an issue of pressure and heat. Good fuel line may take the pressure but it won't like the heat and oil working it. (Pressure in the cooling lines isn't horridly high but the heat is way up there.)<P>You can get tranny line anyplace that sells auxilary tranny coolers. It's definatly the better choice.<P>If you have a leak in a hard line, just patch it with a lenght of rubber tranny line. It's a whole lot easier, and ends up just as reliable.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dan||07-12-2000||06:35 PM|||| It is a gravy job.Easest if you remove  the left rear tire.Look down at the tranny and you'll see where the hose connects to the tranny to the tubing.It is an under and over job.||||reg||1
